  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1217 PM MDT Sun Jul 12 2026\n\nAbove-normal warmth will persist for the foreseeable future with \ndaytime temperatures and heat risk peaking today across Southeast \nIdaho. HEAT ADVISORIES and EXTREME HEAT WARNINGS remain in effect \nthrough Monday evening. We still stand a chance of breaking all-time \nrecords for daytime highs today at Pocatello and Idaho Falls. The \nall-time record high temperatures for our official climate sites are \nas follows: 104 in Pocatello, 102 in Idaho Falls, 107 in Burley, 103 \nin Challis, and 96 in Stanley. Vulnerable populations or anyone \nexposed to the elements for long periods of time could experience \nheat-related illness if precautions are not taken. Overnight lows \ntonight will span the upper-50s to low-60s across the lower \nelevations. \n\nBeginning tomorrow, a trough descending out of Canada will shift the \narea of high pressure slightly further east for the remainder of \nnext week. This will mean a return to daily isolated to scattered \nconvection and slightly cooler, fairly stable temperatures under \nincreased cloud cover. Isolated showers and thunderstorms will \narrive as early as Monday evening. Storms will trend wetter and more \nscattered in coverage heading into the middle of next week as PWATs \nbegin to approach 0.75\" on Monday and just over an inch by \nWednesday.",
